Description: 62_threads-ctx_new-deadlock.t: _exit after passing (teardown heap race)
 The test spawns 20 detached threads doing CTX/SSL/SESSION create+free,
 busy-waits on threads->list(), reports its single pass, and exit(0)s.
 A detached thread can still be mid-teardown when the interpreter
 starts global destruction, and the concurrent OpenSSL 3 frees
 intermittently corrupt the heap — glibc aborts AFTER the pass:
 .
   ok 1 - successfully finished, duration=0
   double free or corruption (fasttop)
   t/local/62_threads-ctx_new-deadlock.t
     (Wstat: 134 (Signal: ABRT, dumped core) Tests: 1 Failed: 0)
 .
 Far likelier on the 48-core Athena builder where the 20 threads run
 truly in parallel.  The deadlock property under test has already been
 verified and reported by that point (Test::Builder autoflushes its
 handles), so finish with POSIX::_exit(0) and skip interpreter global
 destruction entirely.
Origin: vendor, Athena-Build
Forwarded: no
Author: Athena-Build
Last-Update: 2026-11-08
---
--- a/t/local/62_threads-ctx_new-deadlock.t
+++ b/t/local/62_threads-ctx_new-deadlock.t
@@ -30,7 +30,14 @@
 do_sleep(50) while (threads->list());
 
 pass("successfully finished, duration=".(time-$start_time));
-exit(0);
+
+# The test has passed and reported (Test::Builder autoflushes), but
+# detached worker threads may still be mid-teardown: letting the
+# interpreter run global destruction concurrently intermittently
+# corrupts the heap ("double free or corruption (fasttop)", SIGABRT)
+# with OpenSSL 3 on many-core machines.  Skip destruction entirely.
+require POSIX;
+POSIX::_exit(0);
 
